analysis:
Both changed files are test-only: KVPoolIdleSweepTests.swift (new, 377 lines) and ProcessMemoryLedgerTests.swift (+62 lines at head 278-339). No production code, wire protocol, key handling, attestation, authorization or storage paths changed in the supplied units, so no new attack surface or invalidated threat assumption was found.

The tests exercise memory-reclaim behavior that touches TB-007 (in-process inference engine) and T-028 (residual GPU/KV data) and T-029 (resource monopolization):
- idle/pressure/closed sweep windows;
- WholeMacServiceBudget idle detection and performIfIdle admission hold-off;
- ProcessMemoryLedger.capacityShortfall accounting, including debt and overflow (.max) cases;
- kill-switch env vars DARKBLOOM_TTFT_IDLE_CACHE_SWEEP, DARKBLOOM_TTFT_SEGMENT_RETENTION and DARKBLOOM_TTFT_SEGMENT_HOST_ZEROS;
- retained KV segment release with cancellation.

Security-relevant observations for later passes: the tests imply production code adds retained free KV segments (default min(2 GiB, grant/8), 30 s idle) plus host-zeroed segments, with a rollback that disables host zeroing when retention is off. Retained segments keep prior-request KV memory parked inside the process. That matters for T-028 (residual data) and the cross-request leakage posture, so the production diff (not supplied here) should confirm that segments are zeroed or overwritten before reuse across tenants and that the device-fill path (HOST_ZEROS=0) still clears them. Also, rollbacks are env-var driven, which is operator-local. That is consistent with the existing TB-003 assumptions and not a new boundary. Nothing in the tests touches the T-041 SSD cache crypto, HMAC names or TTL. The production sources (KVPoolReclaimer, WholeMacServiceBudget, ProcessMemoryLedger, PagedKVPool, EngineV2Factory) were not supplied, so I cannot verify their behavior; this is a coverage limit, not evidence of safety.
